## Coverage
src/api.rs 1-893 full read. Also: src/rpc.rs 20-113, 695-715; src/config.rs 208-235, 310-321; src/provider/mod.rs 134-140; src/provider/gemini.rs 286-296 (key via x-goog-api-key header, not URL). No cargo runs (read-only audit).

## Findings (3, all Low)

### 1. [Low] Raw upstream errors (incl. internal upstream URL) echoed to clients
- Location: src/api.rs:476-482, 541-547, 697-703, 726-732, and SSE Err branch ~620-624.
- Evidence (api.rs:477-482):
```rust
        Err(e) => openai_error(
            StatusCode::BAD_GATEWAY,
            &format!("upstream error: {e}"),
            "server_error",
        ),
```
- Scenario: on upstream connect/TLS failure, reqwest::Error's Display includes the full URL ('error sending request for url (https://internal-gateway.corp/v1/chat/completions)'). Returned verbatim in the 502 body, letting an authenticated (or loopback-open unauthenticated) client scout internal upstream hosts, model paths, and topology. Secrets are sent as headers and not leaked, hence Low.
- Fix: return a fixed message; log `e` server-side via warn!.

### 2. [Low] constant_time_eq early-return on length mismatch — token-length timing oracle
- Location: src/config.rs:311-314 (callers src/api.rs:870-873, src/rpc.rs:38-44).
- Evidence (config.rs:311-317):
```rust
pub fn constant_time_eq(a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> bool {
    if a.len() != b.len() {
        return false;
    }
    let mut diff = 0u8;
    for (x, y) in a.iter().zip(b.iter()) {
```
- Scenario: timing probes across candidate Bearer lengths show a measurable time step at the true token length (XOR loop runs only on length match). The function's own doc promises no timing oracle, so this violates its contract; practical exploitability low.
- Fix: pad to max length and XOR the whole range, ORing the length difference into diff.

### 3. [Low] rate_buckets eviction holds the shared mutex for O(n^2) work
- Location: src/api.rs:781-796.
- Evidence (api.rs:781-794):
```rust
    let mut buckets = state.rate_buckets.lock().await;
    if buckets.len() > 1024 {
        buckets.retain(|_, (_, last)| last.elapsed() < Duration::from_secs(300));
        while buckets.len() > 1024 {
            let Some((&oldest, _)) = buckets.iter().min_by_key(|(_, (_, last))| *last) else {
                break;
            };
            buckets.remove(&oldest);
        }
    }
```
- Scenario: sustained traffic from >1024 distinct IPs makes every request run a full min_by_key scan (1024 comparisons) per removal inside the mutex, stalling all /v1, /ws, /metrics requests behind the lock. Bounded by the 1024 cap, hence Low.
- Fix: collect (ip,last) once, use select_nth_unstable/partial sort for the cutoff, batch-retain.

## Clean (dup-exclusion material for next audit)
- ws_ticket one-shot: issuance (api.rs:317-320, 32B OS RNG) vs consumption (rpc.rs:48-53, atomic Mutex remove) correct, expiry rechecked, no concurrent-reuse race.
- auth_token_cache: re-resolve on raw mismatch; Err cached and fail-closed (503 for None/Err on non-loopback); empty-resolved token rejected; config rechecked before cache store prevents stale overwrite.
- require_token: fail-closed on non-loopback after reload drops token; loopback no-token allows only loopback Origin (is_localhost_origin treats null/unparseable as non-loopback).
- Both auth sites strip 'Bearer ' then constant_time_eq; resolution-failure details go only to server-side warn! logs.
- rate_limit: no-op when ConnectInfo absent (no spoofable headers); bucket refill math correct; loopback-bind skip intentional.
- /metrics gated by require_token on non-loopback; exposes counters only.
- openai_error itself exposes fixed message+kind only (issue is the format! call sites).
- forward does not relay client Authorization/cookies upstream; provider key injected server-side.
- Promote recursion capped by depth param; passthrough path non-recursive; promoted error body capped at 1 MiB with 30s timeout.
- Router order: /metrics rate_limit outer then require_token inner; /ws also rate-limited (blocks online token brute-force).

## Notes
- rpc.rs:52 hardcodes a 60s ticket TTL instead of WS_TICKET_TTL — same value today, behavioral non-defect, excluded from findings.
- Provider internals (collect_stream, RateLimited), store, relay are outside this assignment.
